# Repository-wide default. Text is normalized to LF in the repository.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Anything executed by a shell MUST check out with LF, on every platform.
#
# A shell script written from a Windows checkout gets CRLF, and the kernel then reads the shebang
# as "/usr/bin/env bash\r" - a program that does not exist. The error is
#
#     env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# which names neither the file nor line endings. CI never sees it, because a GitHub runner checks
# out on Linux and gets LF either way - so this only bites when someone deploys or runs a script
# from a Windows working copy, which is exactly when they are least expecting a tooling problem.
# It has cost this project three separate afternoons, in three different repositories, which is
# why the rule is now stated once for all of them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.sh        text eol=lf
install.sh  text eol=lf

# Read by Linux tooling, never by a Windows one.
*.desktop   text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.conf      text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The mirror image of the rule above: read by a Windows shell, never by a Linux one.
#
# `* text=auto eol=lf` would check these out with LF. cmd.exe tolerates that for a one-line
# script and stops tolerating it around `goto`, labels and multi-line blocks, where it consumes
# the label including the missing CR and jumps nowhere. That failure names neither the file nor
# line endings either, and it only appears once a wrapper grows past a single command - which is
# to say, later, when nobody is looking for it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.ps1       text eol=crlf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Committed binaries - never touched by any line-ending or diff heuristic.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.ico       binary
*.svg       text eol=lf
*.woff2     binary
*.ttf       binary
*.jar       binary
*.zip       binary
*.tar.gz    binary
*.pdf       binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT set here, deliberately: an explicit `*.java text eol=lf`.
#
# The Java sources came across the merge with CRLF endings, and adding that rule would renormalize
# every one of them - a whole-tree whitespace commit that buries real changes and rewrites blame
# for files nobody touched. The `* text=auto eol=lf` above already normalizes anything written
# from here on.
#
# It is worth knowing why this matters. Promoting the ex-tenant cleanup surfaced a diff of 3,094
# lines on SmartChatMemory.java of which exactly ONE was real - a comment - the rest being trailing
# whitespace an editor stripped on save. Both sides were CRLF, so it was not an EOL flip, but the
# effect is identical: a one-line change made unreviewable. If that recurs often enough to be
# worth a whole-tree renormalization, do it as its own commit that touches nothing else, and say
# so in the message.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Signed fixtures: a signature covers exact bytes, so git must never touch their line endings.
apps/app/backend/proxyma-core-update/src/test/resources/signing/** -text
